### What is a Kafka partition and why does count matter?

A Kafka topic is divided into partitions, and each partition is an ordered, immutable sequence of records. The partition count matters because it caps consumer parallelism. In one consumer group a partition is owned by exactly one consumer, so a topic with six partitions can have at most six actively consuming instances in a group. A seventh consumer sits idle. This means the partition count is the ceiling on how many instances can consume in parallel.

In a banking context, the `transactions` topic at National Bank of India might receive thousands of fund transfer events per second. If that topic has only two partitions, only two settlement service instances can process in parallel, regardless of how many pods Kubernetes has launched.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class TransferProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());

        // The topic "transfers" has 12 partitions, so up to 12 consumers can work in parallel
        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", "SAV2001", "DEBIT 50000"));
            System.out.println("Sent transfer event for account SAV2001");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Sent transfer event for account SAV2001
```

### How do you calculate the right partition count?

The sizing formula is practical. You estimate target throughput, say sixty megabytes per second, and the throughput a single partition sustains for your consumers, say ten megabytes per second limited by downstream processing. Dividing gives six partitions as a floor, and you add headroom, often doubling, to allow future consumer scaling without a repartition.

The same reasoning applies to message rate rather than bytes if your bottleneck is records per second.

- **Floor** = `targetThroughput / perPartitionThroughput`
- **With headroom** = `floor * 2` (typical)
- **Example** = 60 MB/s / 10 MB/s = 6, doubled to 12 partitions

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class PartitionCalculator {
    public static int calculate(long targetMBps, long perPartitionMBps, double headroom) {
        int floor = (int) Math.ceil((double) targetMBps / perPartitionMBps);
        int withHeadroom = (int) Math.ceil(floor * headroom);
        System.out.println("Floor partitions: " + floor);
        System.out.println("With headroom (" + headroom + "x): " + withHeadroom);
        return withHeadroom;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // National Bank of India transfers topic sizing
        int partitions = calculate(60, 10, 2.0);
        System.out.println("Recommended partitions for transfers topic: " + partitions);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Floor partitions: 6
With headroom (2.0x): 12
Recommended partitions for transfers topic: 12
```

### What are the broker-side constraints on partition count?

Each partition replica is a set of files on a broker, so more partitions mean more open file handles and more memory for index and page cache. A broker has a practical ceiling on total partitions, often in the low thousands per broker depending on version and hardware, and the cluster has a ceiling in the tens to low hundreds of thousands.

Too many partitions also slow down controller operations, lengthen rebalances, and increase end-to-end latency because the producer spreads batches thinner so each batch fills more slowly. More partitions also increase the time to recover after a broker failure because more leader elections must happen.

| Constraint | Impact | Typical Limit |
|---|---|---|
| Open file handles | OS limit per broker | ~4000-10000 partitions per broker |
| Memory (indexes + page cache) | Broker heap and OS memory | Depends on hardware |
| Controller operations | Metadata updates slow | Tens of thousands cluster-wide |
| Rebalance time | Consumer group pauses | Proportional to partition count |
| Recovery time | Leader elections per partition | Minutes at high counts |
| End-to-end latency | Batches fill slower | Measurable above ~100 partitions |

